I agree with the direction, with one correction to keep it from becoming too
broad: the graph function is not "best fit for a->g" in the abstract. It is best
fit for the current target obligation binding at the current frame.

The lawful selection input is:

```text
current frame
+ product asset model
+ observed gap pressure
+ target obligation binding
+ published action catalog
+ visible policy
```

That matters because `a->g` is too coarse by itself. If the system selects
against the whole outer path, it can recreate broad fallback. If it selects
against the exact binding, then `conform_project_authority(a):b` is selected
because `b` is the current missing authority asset, not because it is generally
useful.

## 1. Frame stack, not hidden nested control

The right mental model is a frame stack:

```text
OuterFrame: a -> g
  selected action: conform_project_authority(a):b
  ChildFrame: a1 -> a2
    selected action: graph_function(x):y
```

Each published child frame gets the full loop:

```text
synthesize_model
-> eval_gap
-> bind target obligation
-> evaluate_next
-> admit ConstructionIntent
-> invoke
-> admit evidence
-> evaluate_action
-> close/yield/retry/repair/re-enter/reprice/block
```

The parent frame does not inspect the child's private implementation. It consumes
the child's admitted closure/evidence as evidence for the parent target. That is
the line between lawful recursion and hidden orchestration.

If the inner work is unpublished implementation detail, it can use local control
flow as ordinary realization code. It cannot dispatch graph work, retry graph
work, repair graph work, or close graph work as a hidden controller. The moment
it is a published vector or candidate action, it is back under the loop.

## 2. Typed inheritance needs three compatibility checks

The `x <-inherits- a1` and `y <-inherits- a2` idea is right, but I would avoid a
single "subtype match" rule. A candidate graph action should be admissible only
when three checks pass:

```text
input compatibility: current asset can lawfully satisfy candidate input
output compatibility: candidate output can lawfully satisfy target obligation
evidence compatibility: candidate proof/evidence can fold into parent closure
```

This is what prevents a type match from becoming another broad action selector.
The output side is as important as the input side. `graph_function(x):y` is not
lawful merely because `x` refines `a1`; it is lawful because `y` refines or
fulfills the exact target asset expected at that frame.

For ranking, specificity is a policy signal, not the whole law:

```text
admissible first
then exact target binding
then most specific type fit
then policy priority
then deterministic tie-break
```

## 3. CandidateFamily is the right publication shape

This thought experiment makes `CandidateFamily` more important than a plain
function list.

The catalog should be able to say:

```text
for target obligation T at frame F:
  candidates = [
    conform_project_authority,
    repair_project_authority,
    materialize_authority_shell,
    clarify_project_authority
  ]
```

`evaluate_next` ranks those candidates. The runner invokes only the admitted
ConstructionIntent that cites the selected row. A graph function can still be a
named public carrier, but selection happens over an action row inside a published
family.

That keeps the operator model clean:

- graph function: constructive carrier
- candidate family: lawful choice set
- evaluate_next: deterministic selector over the choice set
- ConstructionIntent: admitted invocation record

## 4. Evaluator callout returns findings, not authority

The evaluator-as-configured-callout framing is good, but the callout should not
be the authority surface by itself.

The safe shape is:

```text
configured evaluator callout -> findings / ranking proposal
ABG/F_D admission -> PriorityProjection / NextActionProjection
NextActionProjection -> ConstructionIntent admission
```

That preserves product configurability without letting the configured evaluator
invent graph actions or bypass ABG admission. Product policy can rank and prefer;
published GTL/ABG action authority still decides what can be invoked.

## 5. Evidence lifting is the closure rule

The parent frame needs a named way to consume child results:

```text
Child EdgeClosureDecision(close)
+ child output asset refs
+ child evidence refs
+ child lineage refs
-> parent AdmittedEvidence row
```

Without that lifting rule, recursion will leak. Parent `evaluate_action` will
either inspect child internals directly or trust a summary string. Both break the
one-surface rule.

So the recursive version needs one additional carrier concept, even if the name
changes later:

```text
FrameClosureEvidence
```

It says: this child frame closed, these target assets were produced or refined,
these refs prove it, and this is the parent frame obligation it satisfies.

## 6. What this implies for the current main loop

The loop becomes:

```text
observe current workspace state
-> synthesize product asset model
-> eval_gap
-> bind exact target obligation
-> evaluate_next over published candidate family
-> admit construction intent
-> invoke selected graph function
-> if selected graph function publishes refinement, open child frame
-> child frame runs the same loop
-> child closure lifts into parent evidence
-> parent evaluate_action closes/yields/repairs/re-enters
-> evaluate_next selects next action
```

This preserves the premise:

```text
observe reality
-> compare to model
-> discover gap
-> synthesize current construction intent from gap
-> select lawful graph action
-> close the gap
```

It also keeps the key separation: intent does not create actions. Intent seeds
the model. Gap binds model to reality. `evaluate_next` selects among already
published lawful actions. ConstructionIntent admits the selected action for one
bounded invocation.

## 7. Tests this design would eventually need

- A published child vector must run through the same loop, not a private runner.
- An opaque implementation may use local control flow but cannot dispatch a
  hidden graph action.
- A candidate with matching input type but incompatible output target is
  rejected.
- Two admissible candidates with different type specificity rank
  deterministically.
- Child closure cannot close the parent unless lifted evidence cites the parent
  target obligation.
- Parent retry/resume context is derived from frame/closure/projection refs, not
  from child prose or gap-dossier strings.

Net: the thread is pointing at a strong abstraction. I would phrase it as
"recursive typed action selection over published frame obligations" rather than
"graph function is best fit for a->g." The first version preserves exact target
binding; the second version is true only when `a->g` has already been narrowed to
the current frame obligation.
